---
source: tests/help_snapshots.rs
---
Show the core-hours that recent runs used, newest first.

Core-hours are the unit that Antithesis bills usage in, and that the usage cap
counts. They are not wall-clock hours: Antithesis runs a test in parallel, so
one hour of testing uses many core-hours, and a higher performance tier uses
more.

Each run has its ID, the start and end of its billable usage, and its
core-hours.

Columns: RUN ID, STARTED, ENDED, CORE HOURS. CORE HOURS has one decimal place.

With --rollup, each row is the total of one day, week, or month: its first and
last days, its number of runs, and its core-hours. A run counts in the period in
which it ended. A week starts on Monday. Columns: START, END, RUNS, CORE HOURS.
The rows are the --limit periods that end with the current period, and a period
with no runs shows 0.

Times and periods are in the local timezone.

--after and --before select runs by the time that they ended: --after includes a
run that ended at that time, and --before does not. Each takes a date, which is
the start of that day, a time such as `2026-10-01 12:00:00`, or an RFC 3339
time. A date or a time without a zone is in the local timezone. With --rollup, a
period is in the output when it holds a selected time. When a filter cuts a
period, the total of that period includes only the runs inside the filter.

--limit counts rows: runs, or periods with --rollup. The default is 10. The
filters select which rows the limit keeps:
  no filter or --before    the newest rows (before --before)
  --after                  the oldest rows from --after
  --after and --before     every row between them; --limit is not allowed
The rows always show newest first.

Examples:
  snouty runs usage
  snouty runs usage --rollup week -n 4
  snouty runs usage --rollup month --after 2026-01-01
  snouty runs usage --after 2026-10-01 --before 2026-11-01

Add --json for machine-readable output. Each row prints as one JSON object on
its own line. The hours field holds core-hours. A run has the fields run_id,
hours, start_time, and end_time; the times are RFC 3339 with the local UTC
offset. A period has the fields date, runs, and hours, with hours rounded to
hundredths:
  snouty --json runs usage --rollup month | jq -r '"\(.date) \(.hours)"'

Usage: snouty runs usage [OPTIONS]

Options:
      --rollup <ROLLUP>
          Total the runs of each day, week, or month
          
          [possible values: day, week, month]

      --after <AFTER>
          Only show runs that ended at or after this date (e.g. 2026-10-01),
          time (e.g. 2026-10-01 12:00:00) or RFC 3339 time

      --before <BEFORE>
          Only show runs that ended before this date (e.g. 2026-11-01), time
          (e.g. 2026-11-01 12:00:00) or RFC 3339 time

  -n, --limit <LIMIT>
          Maximum number of rows to display: runs, or periods with a rollup
          [default: 10; not allowed with both an after and a before time]

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

      --json
          Output JSON where supported (NDJSON for list/stream commands, pretty
          JSON otherwise)

      --verbose
          Log API requests to stderr (authentication tokens redacted)

      --settings <SETTINGS>
          Path to the snouty settings file (default: ./.snouty.toml; overrides
          SNOUTY_SETTINGS_PATH)

      --profile <PROFILE>
          Settings profile to select (overrides ANTITHESIS_PROFILE)
